.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-key-escrow-kit 1  "tapectl-key-escrow-kit " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-key\-escrow\-kit \- Write the Heir Kit: the cover sheet, its catalog and this tapectl
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl key escrow\-kit\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-static\-binary\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIDIR\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Write the Heir Kit: the cover sheet, its catalog and this tapectl
.PP
The printed Heir Kit and the encrypted catalog bundle (ADR\-0005, ADR\-0009). Writes four files: COVER.txt (the plain\-text cover sheet — the artifact with the decades\-scale claim), escrow\-kit.html (the same content with an inline QR, for printing), catalog.db.age (the whole catalog encrypted to the escrow recipient), and tapectl\-VERSION (a copy of this tapectl program, mode 0755, whose size and sha256 the cover sheet names, so the kit does not depend on a repository surviving).
.PP
A tapectl that is not static runs only where its C library does. With \-\-static\-binary, the release\*(Aqs static build goes in beside it as tapectl\-VERSION\-x86_64\-linux\-musl and the sheet names it first. Without it, the sheet names that release asset\*(Aqs URL. When this tapectl is itself static, its copy is the static binary.
.PP
This command stops at the files. Printing them, sealing them into tamper\-evident envelopes and distributing them across at least two independent failure domains is the operator\*(Aqs part, and the cover sheet states those requirements. Re\-run after each production write session; `audit` warns when the kit has fallen behind the tapes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-static\-binary\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
The release\*(Aqs static tapectl, to put in the kit beside this one

The tapectl\-VERSION\-x86_64\-linux\-musl asset of this version\*(Aqs GitHub release. It must be a static x86_64 program whose \-\-version is this tapectl\*(Aqs; the kit copies it in under that name (mode 0755) and the cover sheet names it first, with its size and sha256. Not copied when this tapectl is itself static.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fIDIR\fR>
Directory to write the kit into (created if absent, mode 0700)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
  # After a write session: the kit again, to print and seal
  tapectl key escrow\-kit /mnt/usb/heir\-kit
  # Under the service user
  tapectl\-op key escrow\-kit /srv/archive_meta/heir\-kit
  # With the release\*(Aqs static build, downloaded and checked against SHA256SUMS
  tapectl key escrow\-kit /mnt/usb/heir\-kit \-\-static\-binary ./tapectl\-VERSION\-x86_64\-linux\-musl
.fi
